# Decision Framework

Before implementation, engineers evaluate

Business impact

Maintenance cost

Security

Scalability

Complexity

Performance

User experience

Developer experience

Statistical correctness

When multiple solutions are acceptable, choose the solution with the lowest long-term complexity.

---

# Progress Reporting

Maintain an engineering journal.

Each iteration records

Current milestone

Completed work

Active work

Blocked work

Known risks

Technical debt

Next iteration

Reports should summarize progress.

Do not emit verbose implementation logs.

---

# Escalation Rules

Routine engineering decisions should not interrupt execution.

Escalation is appropriate only when

Business requirements are ambiguous.

Multiple architectures have meaningful tradeoffs.

Public APIs materially change.

Security boundaries materially change.

Infrastructure costs materially change.

Product behavior materially changes.

External credentials are required.

---

# Autonomous Engineering

Engineers are expected to independently decide

Folder structure

Naming

Validation

Logging

Testing

Refactoring

Dependency organization

Documentation

Error handling

These decisions should not require approval.

---

# Quality Gates

Every engineering iteration executes

Linting

Formatting

Type checking

Unit tests

Integration tests

Contract tests

Migration validation

Performance validation

Security validation

Documentation validation

Failures prevent milestone completion.

---

# Definition of Completion

A milestone is complete only when

All planned work is implemented.

Quality gates pass.

Documentation is current.

Architecture decisions are recorded.

Technical debt is documented.

No known regressions remain.

Remaining work has been added to the backlog.

---

# Stop Conditions

Engineering stops only when

A product decision requires human judgment.

A major architectural tradeoff requires human judgment.

External credentials are required.

Requirements are insufficient.

The milestone satisfies all acceptance criteria.

Routine engineering work should never trigger a stop.

